The release workflow runs on GitHub Actions; pushes, PRs and crons run on
Woodpecker, which is all ci-debug could read. Its GitHub arm still said
"GitHub Actions is disabled", so a release run, a workflow_dispatch dry
run and its artifacts were invisible through the approved scripts, and on
2026-10-17 the owner had to waive the CI-API rule once for `gh`.

Explicit `--actions`, anywhere on the line; the default arm is unchanged:

  runs --actions [workflow.yml] [N]   /actions/runs or /actions/workflows/<f>/runs
  status --actions <run-id>           /actions/runs/<id> + its jobs: steps, durations
  logs --actions <run-id> [job]       /actions/jobs/<job>/logs (first failed job by default)
  artifact --actions <run-id> <name> <new-empty-dir>
                                      /actions/runs/<id>/artifacts + /actions/artifacts/<a>/zip

JSON goes through api_get. Logs and artifact zips come back as a redirect
to a storage host; ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md says such a redirect is not
followed unless the host is listed, so _actions_download checks the target
against that file and, when unlisted, names it and exits 3. Measured live:
both redirect to productionresultssa0.blob.core.windows.net, which is not
listed, so today `runs` and `status` work and `logs`/`artifact` refuse by
design; whether to list that host is the owner's call (the file says so).

Artifacts extract only into a NEW empty directory, and a zip member that
escapes it is refused before anything is written.

The fake curl in tests/_forge_github_harness.py learns redirects
(`location`), `-L`, `-w` formatting of %{redirect_url}, and binary bodies
(`body_b64`); existing harness users are unchanged (75 pass).

Owner approval for this governance change: WI-pubur discussion, 2026-10-17
("i approve WI-pubur and approve auto-pr without --gov").

